--TEST--
`Core\Task\Channel::close` ends the stream without discarding it, is idempotent, and leaves a drained channel walking zero times — `nvs_stdlib::channel`'s end-of-stream, `rule:iteration/two-interfaces`'s iteration protocol
--FILE--
<?nvs
// No tasks anywhere in this case, deliberately: nothing here reaches the
// bound, so nothing here waits, and a channel that needs a scheduler to be
// *used* would be a worse type than one that only needs a scheduler to wait.
var $chan = new Core\Task\Channel<string>(3);

$chan->send("a");
$chan->send("b");

// Closing does not discard: a value sent before the close is delivered after
// it. The second call asks for a state the channel is already in, which is not
// an error — there is nothing a diagnostic could help with.
$chan->close();
$chan->close();

foreach ($chan as string $queued) {
    echo $queued, "\n";
}

// Empty *and* closed is the end of the walk rather than a wait, so a second
// `foreach` over the same channel runs its body zero times instead of hanging.
foreach ($chan as string $queued) {
    echo "unexpected ", $queued, "\n";
}

echo "drained", "\n";
--EXPECT--
a
b
drained
